From 6a1cbff42291cda7113482396ec1ef5edc5a8c54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 09:55:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Spare proven open core publication owners from spend
 triage

Why: the home budget pass correctly enforces cumulative spend for remotely
leased cards, but David subsequently required open core PR owners to remain
eligible while their publication is still being landed. An ordinary PR mention
or review must not purchase an exemption.

Use published_pr run metadata or an owning-run canonical-ship publication
report, then the existing cached gh read's raw state and base. Only OPEN
example-org/hermes main PRs qualify. Read GitHub only after the
budget is exceeded, outside the write transaction. Recheck spend, ownership
and lane/claim state under lock. Record one spend_budget_spared notice per
window without resetting accounting. No-progress and pending triage retain
priority; state PRs and unknown lookups receive normal spend enforcement.

Proof: RED on fresh main: open owner triaged (1 failed / 10 controls passed).
GREEN final consumer suite: 70 files, 1669 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped;
52 focused spare cases include both dispatcher paths and real publication
shapes from read-only hub replay. Ten targeted mutants all killed. Full
scripts/check: 21 passed. Prepublication independent inspection found quoted
review and reopened-CLOSED cache gaps; added regressions and fixed both.

Read-only replay checked nine temporarily exempt hub cards: genuine canonical
publication/metadata evidence found for five; four carried only different
handoff/review shapes or no evidence and remain unproven, not silently spared.
No live exemptions or statuses were changed. Required exact-head independent
review and CI still gate landing; live unflagged spare and exemption cleanup
follow normal deployment. The original home-pass race remains: a worker can
lease between ready and the home's next tick, allowing one extra run per
episode. GitHub OPEN/base observation uses the existing five-minute cache.
